A. Settings
Datasets. Experiments are performed on four image classification benchmark datasets (example images are shown in Fig. 2 and dataset statistics are in Table I): (i) Office-31 [40], which contains 4, 110 images from 31 classes of three domains (Amazon (A), DSLR (D), and Webcam (W)). Six transfer tasks (A→W, D→W, W→D, A→D, D→A, W→A) are constructed. (ii) Office-Home [41], which contains 15, 500 images from 65 classes of four domains (Art (Ar), Clipart (Cl), Product (Pr), and Real-World (Rw)). All combinations of domain transfer are considered, leading to a total of 12 transfer tasks. (iii) VisDA-2017 [42], which is a large-scale synthetic-to-real dataset with 207, 785 images from 12 classes of two domains (Synthetic and Real). Following [10], [13], [20], we consider the transfer task Synthetic → Real. (iv) miniDomainNet [43], which is a subset of DomainNet [44], with 140, 006 images from 126 classes of four domains (Clipart (C), Painting (P), Real (R), Sketch (S)). Following [45], 12 transfer tasks are constructed. Baselines. We compare with ERM [39] and a number of UDA methods, including (i) discrepancy-based methods: AFN [23], MDD [10], MSGD [24], MCC [20], and (ii) adversarial-based methods: DANN [21], CDAN [26], BDG [16], SDAT [13], ELS [14]. We combine the proposed DACDM with the stateof-the-art in these two categories (namely, MCC and ELS). Note that the same UDA method is used in label-conditioned training and domain-guided generation, though in general they can be different.
Implementation Details. To initialize εθ, we use the CDPM in [30], which is pretrained on ImageNet and has a U-Net [32] architecture. The resolutions of generated images are 128 × 128 for the miniDomainNet dataset, and 256 × 256 for the other three datasets. And we use an image size of 96×96 in miniDomainNet dataset [43], and 256 × 256 for the other three datasets [14], [20]. The images in the source and target domains are processed to the same resolution as the generated images. For each class, 200, 200, 2, 000, and 200 images are generated for Office-31, Office-Home, VisDA-2017, and miniDomainNet, respectively. For the backbones of the UDA models, following [13], [43], ResNet-50 [46] is used for both Office-31 and Office-Home datasets, while ResNet-101 and ResNet-18 are used for the VisDA-2017 and miniDomainNet datasets, respectively. The learning rate scheduler follows [21]. For ELS+DACDM, the initial learning rate is 0.002 for Office31 and VisDA-2017, and 0.01 for the other two datasets. As for MCC+DACDM, the initial learning rate is 0.002 for VisDA2017, and 0.005 for the other three datasets. All experiments are run on an NVIDIA V100 GPU.
